<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierBlockedCapability> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "frontier label: blocked on capability or resource"@x-gmeow-english .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierBlockedCapability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Derives logic:FrontierBlockedCapabilityOrResource for a frontier entry whose step axis is waiting and whose action is the blocked step of an operational capability gap. The gap is an operational one — a capability the deployment does not have — and never a reasoning-profile gap, so this rule is also where the non-conflation is enforced by construction rather than by a reviewer noticing."@x-gmeow-english .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierBlockedCapability> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/provenance> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierBlockedCapability> .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierCompensationEligible> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "frontier label: compensation eligible"@x-gmeow-english .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierCompensationEligible> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Derives logic:FrontierCompensationEligible for a frontier entry whose effect axis sits at receipted — a forward effect is known to have committed, so a counteraction is now permissible. Eligibility is all this derives: it asserts no compensation attempt, no compensation outcome, and no claim that compensating will succeed, because compensation is a new action with its own possibility of failing."@x-gmeow-english .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierCompensationEligible> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/provenance> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierCompensationEligible> .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReadyApprovalRequired> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "frontier label: ready but approval required"@x-gmeow-english .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReadyApprovalRequired> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Derives logic:FrontierReadyApprovalRequired for a frontier entry whose step axis is ready and whose approval axis is created but not yet detached. Readiness and approval are separate axes, and this rule is where that separation earns its keep: the same entry is simultaneously ready to run and gated, which a single blended state could only express by minting a combined value for every such pairing."@x-gmeow-english .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReadyApprovalRequired> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/provenance> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReadyApprovalRequired> .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReadyAuthorized> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "frontier label: ready and authorized"@x-gmeow-english .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReadyAuthorized> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Derives logic:FrontierReadyAuthorized for a frontier entry whose step axis is ready and whose approval axis is null — the policy already authorizes the effect, so no operator gate stands between the action and its dispatch. Positive Horn. This is the rule that makes policy-maximal automation a derivation rather than a habit: an entry is authorized because its axis tuple says so, not because nobody remembered to gate it."@x-gmeow-english .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReadyAuthorized> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/provenance> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReadyAuthorized> .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReconciliationRequired> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "frontier label: reconciliation required"@x-gmeow-english .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReconciliationRequired> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Derives logic:FrontierReconciliationRequired for a frontier entry whose effect axis sits at attempted — an effect was dispatched and no receipt settled it. This is the no-blind-retry law stated positively: rather than only forbidding a retry, the frontier NAMES the one action that is licensed from an undetermined position, so an operator reading it is told what to do instead of merely what not to do."@x-gmeow-english .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReconciliationRequired> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/provenance> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/ruleFrontierReconciliationRequired> .
